WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.270
Good coordination starts with a clear intelligence requirement. A requirement states the

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.770
decision to be supported,

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:09.360
the audience, the timeframe, and the information needed to answer the question.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.595
It turns a broad topic into a focused analytic task. Without a clear requirement,

00:00:13.595 --> 00:00:15.430
teams can collect too much, share too widely,

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.640
or confuse interesting information with necessary information.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.279
For example,

00:00:19.279 --> 00:00:23.455
a requirement might ask whether a supplier risk indicator affects currently used vendors,

00:00:23.455 --> 00:00:26.200
whether a fraud pattern is appearing across business units,

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.390
or whether a cyber threat report is relevant to the organization's exposed services.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.030
Each requirement implies a different audience, data need,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.635
and urgency. A technical operations team may need actionable indicators and affected

00:00:37.635 --> 00:00:38.355
assets.

00:00:38.355 --> 00:00:40.400
Executives may need risk, impact, confidence,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.130
and decisions required. Legal or privacy reviewers may need source, authority, retention,

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:46.595
and handling detail.

00:00:46.595 --> 00:00:50.195
Collection discipline means gathering only what is authorized, relevant, proportionate,

00:00:50.195 --> 00:00:51.500
and useful for the decision.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.785
It does not mean collecting every available log, every identity record,

00:00:54.785 --> 00:00:58.185
every partner note, or every personal identifier just in case.

00:00:58.185 --> 00:01:02.260
More data can increase privacy risk, make sharing harder, create retention burdens,

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.760
and dilute analytic focus.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.900
The safest intelligence product often answers the decision question without exposing raw

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.495
sensitive material.

00:01:09.495 --> 00:01:12.970
Minimization begins at the requirements stage. If the decision can be supported with

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:13.850
aggregated counts,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.760
sanitized case summaries, pseudonymous identifiers, or a narrow time window,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.275
the team should not default to full raw datasets.

00:01:20.275 --> 00:01:21.930
If a partner only needs a trend,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.470
do not send names. If an operational team only needs affected systems,

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.120
do not include unrelated personal details.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.190
If legal review is needed before using a data source,

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.220
the requirement should name that condition.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.035
A strong requirement is also reviewable. It records assumptions, scope limits,

00:01:36.035 --> 00:01:38.605
data sources, expected output, caveats, and owner.

00:01:38.605 --> 00:01:39.750
If the question changes,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.380
the requirement should be updated rather than silently expanding collection.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.550
That discipline keeps intelligence work aligned with authority, privacy,

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:47.480
and decision support.
